<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cudjoe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 1720s–1730s</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Captain Cudjoe, Kojo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cudjoe was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-maroons">Leeward Maroons</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, communities of escaped enslaved Africans in the Cockpit Country. He led their resistance in the First Maroon War until, in 1739, he signed a treaty with the British governor Edward Trelawny that granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> land and self-government in return for returning runaways and aiding the colony against invasion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cudjoe.jpg" alt="Cudjoe" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Engravers E. and J. Smith, after E. Smith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CUDJOE_MAKING_PEACE_WITH_COLONEL_JOHN_GUTHRIE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The name Cudjoe corresponds to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> day name Kojo or Kwadwo, given to a boy born on a Monday. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-maroons">Leeward Maroons</a> most likely emerged in 1690, when a Coromantee rebellion broke out on Sutton's estate in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and most of the enslaved Africans there ran away into the mountainous interior. Cudjoe was probably the son of one of the leaders of that revolt. The oral history of the Accompong Town Maroons, as told by Milton McFarlane, holds that he was the freeborn son of Naquan, who led those who fled from the estate.</p><p>According to contemporary white enslavers, Cudjoe challenged a Madagascan for the leadership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-maroons">Leeward Maroons</a> in 1720, and by defeating and killing him became their undisputed leader. His community was known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trelawny-town">Cudjoe's Town</a>, or Trelawny Town. For about half a century, until the treaties of 1739 and 1740, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> operated outside direct British control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the First Maroon War of the 1730s the English colonial forces failed to win any significant victory over Cudjoe's Leeward Maroons. The agreement of 1739 recognised them as a semi-autonomous entity and freed their land from taxes. In the 1740s some Leeward Maroons who opposed the treaty rose in revolt, and Cudjoe crushed these risings.</p><p>Two descriptions of him survive from colonial writers. The physician R. C. Dallas, writing half a century after Cudjoe lived, claimed that he was short and stout, with a large lump of flesh on his back. The slaver Thomas Thistlewood, who met him in 1750, noted in his diary that he wore a feathered hat, with a sword at his side and a gun on his shoulder, barefoot and with &quot;somewhat a Majestic look&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>One source claims that Cudjoe died in 1744, but Edward Long and Thistlewood wrote of meeting him in the 1750s and 1760s. Long described him leading his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> in a martial performance at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montego-bay">Montego Bay</a> for Governor Sir William Lyttleton in 1764, and in the same year Thistlewood heard that he had been dead for some time. The treaty had named Accompong as his successor, but Governor Roger Hope Elletson had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trelawny-town">Trelawny Town</a> badge of authority given instead to a Maroon officer named Lewis, leaving Accompong authority over Accompong Town only. Cudjoe Day is celebrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> on the first Monday in January.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cudjoe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maroon towns were run by elected or chosen leaders, among them <b>Cudjoe</b> in the west and Nanny in the east, now a national heroine.</blockquote><a href="/state/jamaican-maroons">Jamaican Maroons</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Colony of Jamaica. Died in Nanny Town, in what is now <a href="/countries/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.07,-76.52&amp;year=1725" aria-label="Nanny Town on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2475 1493 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Cudjoe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2925 1763h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2925 1763h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=18.07,-76.52&amp;year=1725">What was here in 1725? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
